{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_293","type":"agent_message","text":"Done. The task is complete and committed. Here's a summary of what was done and how it was verified.\n\n## What changed\n\nWorking only through the AEP tools (transaction → edits → check → commit), I modified two internal functions in `h12.ledger`:\n\n- **`categories_in_order(entries)`** — its fold now emits one marker string per *valid* entry (`\"u0001\"<category>\"u0002\"`), preserving duplicates so per-category counts are recoverable. Only valid entries contribute.\n- **`report(entries)`** — now:\n  1. sorts the markers (`sort`) so categories are emitted in **lexicographic order** regardless of input order;\n  2. returns exactly `[\"EMPTY\"]` when there are no markers (empty input or no valid entries);\n  3. folds over the sorted markers, emitting one line per category (run-start detection: first marker, or marker differing from its predecessor);\n  4. computes `category = before(strip-prefix(marker, \"u0001\"), \"u0002\")`, `count = len(split(join(markers, \"\"), \"u0001\"+category+\"u0002\")) - 1`, and reuses the untouched `sum_for` for the real sum (negative amounts included, accumulation intact);\n  5. emits exactly `<category>:<count>:<sum>` lines.\n\n`sum_for`, `cat_seen`, `mk_entry`, `print_report`, `main`, and all scenario functions were left unchanged.\n\n## Why this shape\n\nThe AEP surface has no fold-creation tool, cannot declare composite-typed params on new functions, and cannot change a fold's accumulator type or a function's return type — so adding a conventional `count_for(entries, cat)` helper isn't possible. This implementation reuses the one existing vector-accumulating fold over entries and derives counts deterministically, keeping the exported surface (`Entry`, `report`, `main`) intact.\n\n## Verification\n\n- AEP `check_transaction` → `check ok` with zero problems.\n- `/usr/local/bin/alva project check /workspace/alva.toml` → `ok: 1 modules checked`.\n- `/usr/local/bin/alva project build ... --out-dir /workspace/out` generates the full Rust crate successfully. The final `cargo` step can't run in this sandbox because no Rust toolchain is installed here (the binary invokes `cargo` after codegen); the generated source is complete and I verified the emitted `report` logic by direct simulation of the generated code:\n\n```\nsingle   -> food:1:10\nempty    -> EMPTY\ngrouped  -> food:2:25, fuel:1:30\ninvalid  -> food:1:10, fuel:1:5\nnegative -> food:2:2\npermuted -> food:1:5, fuel:1:3\n```\n\nplus arbitrary permuted/negative/accumulating inputs, all producing lexicographic, deterministic output."}}
